<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meuse-Inférieure</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meuse-Inférieure was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the French Republic and Empire from 1795 to 1814, named after the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>. It covered parts of the former Prince-Bishopric of Liège and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Austrian lands, roughly today's Dutch and Belgian Limburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meuse-inferieure.jpg" alt="Meuse-Inférieure" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Excommunicato (original version by Naturals) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meuse-Inf%C3%A9rieure_departement_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
